WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Randomness-safe C starts by choosing the source based on the consequence of prediction, repetition, or bias.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.760
Not every random value is a secret, and not every generator is suitable for every purpose.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.340
Classify the value before choosing the API.

4
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:21.460
Use security-grade platform or approved library sources for security-sensitive values.

5
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:27.400
Do not design or implement your own cryptographic random number generator in application code.

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:37.260
Let the operating system or trusted library handle entropy and generator state, and make your code responsible for requesting bytes correctly and handling failure safely.

7
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:44.980
Avoid predictable seeding, legacy APIs for sensitive values, unchecked random-source failures, and biased range mapping.

8
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.760
Each of these mistakes can weaken an otherwise reasonable design.

9
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:54.140
A small wrapper can centralize the correct behavior and make call sites easier to review.

10
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.260
Keep test randomness separate from production randomness.

11
00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:05.080
Deterministic seeds can be useful in tests, but production security-sensitive values need unpredictability from the approved source.

12
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.020
Configuration should prevent test behavior from leaking into operational use.

13
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:17.380
Finally, protect generated secrets through careful storage, logging discipline, comparison, expiration, and cleanup.

14
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:25.160
Use documentation, tests, review, and operational checks to keep randomness behavior consistent over time.

15
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:31.020
Good randomness engineering is mostly about choosing boring, trusted mechanisms and using them carefully.
